Z||000002||88formula||05-13-2001||08:13 PM||||Never happen huh! The stock 68 Corvette with a 300 horsepower 327 v8 went 0-60 mph in 7.7 seconds and ran the quarter mile in 15.7 seconds. The 327 cubic inch engine never actually made 300 net horsepower though because of the way they rated horsepower back then. Go here and see for your self. <A HREF="http://web.missouri.edu/~apcb20/times.html" TARGET=_blank>http://web.missouri.edu/~apcb20/times.html</A> <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 05-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||p4n1c||05-13-2001||08:36 PM||||That's the baby I want, to bad I'll be filling up every block and a half in the city.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  This is when Olds actually made an engine that made up for the weight of the car unlike mine.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Used to be ride the rocket..  Now it's ride the geriatric mobile.<P>1969   Oldsmobile 4-4-2 HO 455ci<P>0-6 - 5.9s <BR>1/4 - 13.9s<P>1988   Oldsmobile Cutlass Supreme Int. (auto)<P>0-6 - 11.4s<BR>1/4 - 18.3s<P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>1988 Olds Cutlass Supreme International<p>[This message has been edited by p4n1c (edited 05-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||gixxer||05-13-2001||08:40 PM||||The Corvette small block engine options for '68 were both 327s: 300 HP and 350 HP.<BR>Both gross HP ratings. Knock about 70 HP off each for a net figure.<BR>Good luck finding a 375 HP engine as it has the Rochester mechanical fuel injection, don't forget the 11 to 1 CR!<BR>No older small block Chevys back in the good-old-days ever got HP figures higher than 375 HP.<BR>Sorry about that! =(   <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Predator||05-13-2001||08:42 PM||||I'm not trying to start something here, but according to your source.<P>1988   Pontiac Fiero Formula                  8.0            16.0<P>1968   Chevrolet Corvette 327                 7.7            15.6<P>Stock to Stock the Corvette wins. I love my Fiero to death, but I would take a mint 1968 Corvette over a mint 1988 Fiero anyday..<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||7
Z||000006||88formula||05-13-2001||08:52 PM||||Predator I'm not a flame war man. You are right about the cars stock for stock but a 210 horsepower 3.4 is a different story. I am helping my friend build his 327 engine in his 68 Corvette and it is the 300 horsepower version. I drove my friends 68 Corvette and there is no way the engine has 300 horsepower. It feels more like 165 horsepower.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Predator||05-13-2001||09:00 PM||||I agree a modified Fiero could beat a stock 1968 Corvette. I just wanted to make sure we are comparing Apples to Apples. I see a lot of talk about car comparisons here, but a lot of times they don't show that it is a modified Fiero against a stock whatever. No flame war intended here either. Just friendly conversation.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. Everybody has been way to touchy lately about stuff. I hate it when I reply to something, and I have to worry about starting something as well. People can disagree or show different points of view without it being a mean gesture. The 3.4 build sounds cool. I will probably go that route when my 2.8 finally kicks the bucket. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero4_1.jpg"> ||||reg||7
